{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "lineup structural role coverage (organizer, shaper, terminal)",
    "organizer, shaper, terminal role categories",
    "within-team variation in coverage",
    "separability from player talent and role counts",
    "behaviour of talent control in a coverage model"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "Can lineup coverage be reliably measured using structural role labels from the possession graph?",
    "Does coverage vary enough within a team to identify an effect separate from team fixed effects?",
    "Can the effect of coverage be disentangled from total lineup talent and counts of high-usage players?",
    "Is the estimated coefficient stable and well-behaved after adding talent controls?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-024",
      "status_as_recorded": "rejected",
      "finding": "Structural concentration (the inverse of role spread) was not related to defensive vulnerability: rho=+0.079 p=0.088, adequately powered null across 30 franchises."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-001",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "The A1 adaptation falsification produced an apparent effect that was wholly an artifact of label error and mechanical floor, not a true adaptive response."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Inferred defensive scheme labels could not separate real defensive adjustment from the offense executing differently; near-uniform transition matrices were consistent with label noise."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-027",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "After controlling for roster talent, adaptation quality did not approach significance (p=0.275); the design could not separate coaching adaptiveness from overall team quality due to strong confounding and small sample."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-009",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Lineup-specific PPP estimates for star initiators were obtainable with n>=30 possession cells, showing that possession-level matchup analysis is feasible with this data pipeline."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-001",
      "what_failed": "attribution of a PPP lift to adaptive coverage response",
      "why": "label error and mechanical floor artifact (C_artifact)"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "detecting within-game defensive scheme change from inferred labels",
      "why": "inferred labels indistinguishable from execution noise (A_unanswerable, LABEL_ERROR)"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-027",
      "what_failed": "isolating an adaptation effect from roster talent",
      "why": "strong confounding and insufficient sample size (A_unanswerable, CONFOUNDING, SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER)"
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Structural role labels derived from the same possession graph have produced artefacts (label error, mechanical floor) in prior adaptation studies.",
    "Attempts to separate a strategic variable from overall team talent have failed when only team-level variation is available, due to confounding and power limits.",
    "At the team-season level, structural concentration did not predict defensive vulnerability.",
    "Possession-level lineup-specific PPP analysis is technically feasible with samples of 30+ possessions."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether a binary coverage indicator (>=1 organizer, shaper, terminal) varies meaningfully within the same team-season.",
    "The reliability of the organizer/shaper/terminal classification for individual players, especially those with low minutes or split residual-influence values.",
    "Whether coverage is independent of talent and player counts after controlling for fixed effects.",
    "That any coverage effect on offensive efficiency exists or would survive talent control."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "Lineup coverage can be operationalised per stint using player-season roles derived from usage and residual structural influence.",
    "Within-team variation is plausible because teams field different five-man combinations, but it may be sparse if the team's starters always cover all three roles; the variation would then come from bench-heavy stints.",
    "Role labels are generated from a pipeline that has previously yielded label artefacts, so measurement error in the coverage variable is a primary risk.",
    "Talent controls built from on-court metrics available in the data (e.g., total lineup past PPP, or implied player quality from the possession graph) may suffer from circularity if the same data window is used; an out-of-sample role- and talent-estimation procedure is required.",
    "Even with a large possession-level sample, if coverage is highly collinear with the presence of a few key players, the design may reduce to a standard on/off comparison and not isolate the structural role composition effect."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Stability of the organizer/shaper/terminal classification across season splits and across the three seasons required for out-of-sample labels.",
    "Exact prevalence of lineups that lack one of the three roles within teams; we have no prior estimates.",
    "The best available talent proxy from the per-game data and whether it effectively absorbs the between-player quality variation without introducing lookahead.",
    "Whether the assumed invariance of the role classification to the opponent (the lineup is the unit) holds under the structural influence definition."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The historical record of label artefacts (APPA-001, APPA-005) and the failure to separate a strategic variable from talent (APPS-027) shifts the decision from a simple 'proceed' to 'proceed_with_changes' that require label validation blocs, out-of-sample role estimation, and explicit falsification of the coverage indicator."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.65,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Organizer/shaper/terminal labels may be noisy or unstable, as inferred labels were in APPA-001/APPA-005; this would attenuate any coverage effect and could produce artefacts."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Lineup coverage may be largely determined by which star players are on the court; controlling for total talent or player fixed effects could absorb all variation, leaving no independent coverage effect."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "The number of possessions in lineups missing one of the three roles may be very small for some teams, especially if role classification thresholds place many players in the 'no-role' category, reducing power for within-team comparisons."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-PAP-024"
    ],
    "summary": "PAP-024 examined the inverse concept (structural concentration) at the team level and found no link to defensive vulnerability. No study has yet examined the binary lineup coverage of organizer, shaper, terminal within teams or tested its separability from talent."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Role labels derived from structural influence and usage may misclassify players, especially those near the median cutoffs, leading to mis-measured coverage and attenuation bias."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Coverage may be a proxy for the presence of the team's best players; without careful player-level talent controls, the coverage coefficient will capture a talent effect rather than a structural composition effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Lineups without a shaper or organizer may be rare for some teams, yielding imprecise estimates and forcing poolability assumptions across teams."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "If player roles and talent proxies are computed from the same season as the outcome, forward information may inflate the apparent effect of coverage."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "If the study explores multiple thresholds, outcomes, or role definitions without pre-registration, false positive risk will rise."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.15,
    "rejected": 0.10,
    "untestable": 0.20,
    "artifact": 0.35,
    "uncertain": 0.20
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal effect of on-court lineup coverage (binary: at least one organizer, one shaper, one terminal, based on prior-season role labels) on offensive points per possession (PPP), adjusted for total lineup talent proxies and team-season fixed effects.",
    "unit": "One possession, nested in stints (ten-player intervals) and games.",
    "sample": "All non-garbage-time possessions in NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular season and playoffs for which a complete coverage indicator can be constructed (target ~380,000 possessions). Exclude possessions where any of the five offensive players lacks a role label from the prior season.",
    "power": "Assuming 15% of possessions are in uncovered lineups (baserate), ~60,000 possession-level observations in the minority class, with clustering at the lineup-game level. Minimum detectable effect size (Cohen's d) ~0.03 at 80% power with alpha=0.05, which is sufficient for a falsification-oriented descriptive check.",
    "controls": "Total lineup talent (sum of prior-season single-year RAPM or derived net rating per 100 possessions for the five players); count of high-usage (>median) players; indicator for whether a top-2 structural influence player is on the floor; team-season fixed effects; game-quarter-score-margin cubic polynomial.",
    "falsification": "1) Randomly permute role labels within team-season and verify coverage effect disappears. 2) Test coverage on defensive PPP where no structural composition effect is expected. 3) Vary usage and residual-influence thresholds by ±5 percentiles and require sign stability. 4) Placebo test using future-season role labels to rule out lookahead. 5) Show that the coverage coefficient does not increase when the talent proxy is omitted (indicating no severe confounding).",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-specify one primary outcome (offensive PPP) and one coverage definition; all alternative specifications are secondary and reported with Bonferroni adjustment.",
    "decision_rule": "A coverage coefficient that is significant at p < 0.05 after cluster-robust standard errors, increases R^2 by at least 0.002 beyond talent-only model, and survives all falsification tests will be taken as evidence that coverage is separable and talent control behaves; otherwise the approach is considered unreliable.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 per-game files locked as of 2026-07-13; possession graph built from the same play-by-play source."
  }
}}

